<blockquote><p>
Suppose that I am driving at midnight through some desert. My car breaks down. You are a stranger, and the only other driver in this desert. I manage to stop you, and I offer you a great reward if you drive me to my home. I cannot pay you now, but I promise to do so when we reach my home. &#8230; If you drive me to my home, it would be worse for me if I pay you the promised reward. Since I know that I never do what will be worse for me, I know that I would break my promise. Given my inability to lie convincingly, you know this too. You do not believe my promise. I am stranded in the desert throughout the night.
</p></blockquote>
<p>&#8212; Derek Parfit, <a href="https://archive.org/details/trent_0116300637661/page/6/mode/2up"><em>Reasons and Persons</em></a>, 1984</p>
